          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why does type 2 diabetes affect blood sugar in the first place?",
          "answer": "Type 2 diabetes affects blood sugar levels because the body's tissues become resistant to insulin. This means the insulin produced by the pancreas doesn't work as effectively to move glucose from the blood into cells. To try and overcome this resistance, the pancreas initially produces more insulin. However, over time, the pancreas cannot sustain this increased output, leading to persistently high blood glucose levels. This information comes from the General clinical reference (educational summary) document on \"Type 2 diabetes mellitus\".\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
